   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( g \\) be the function defined by \\( g(x) = \\int_{0}^{x} \\left(2\\sin t - 1\\right) \\, dt \\) for \\( 0 < x < 2 \\pi \\). Find the value of \\( x \\) at which \\( g \\) has a relative maximum."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Divide",
         [
            "Multiply",
            5,
            "Pi"
         ],
         6
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, \\( g'(x) = 2\\sin x - 1 \\) for \\( 0 < x < 2 \\pi \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Multiply",
               2,
               [
                  "Sin",
                  "x"
               ]
            ],
            -1
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Set \\( g'(x) = 0 \\): \\( 2\\sin x - 1 = 0 \\). The solutions in \\( 0 < x < 2 \\pi \\) are \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{5 \\pi}{6} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Multiply",
               5,
               "Pi"
            ],
            6
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Only \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{5 \\pi}{6} \\) lie in \\( 0 < x < 2 \\pi \\); \\( x = - \\frac{7 \\pi}{6} \\) is outside the interval on which \\( g \\) is defined, so it is not a critical point of \\( g \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Multiply",
               5,
               "Pi"
            ],
            6
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "\\( g' \\) changes from positive to negative at \\( x = \\frac{5 \\pi}{6} \\) and from negative to positive at \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\), so \\( g \\) has its relative maximum at \\( x = \\frac{5 \\pi}{6} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
